## 10. Impact & Target Use Cases

### 10.1 For NASA Engineering Managers

Test ECLSS architectures under probabilistic failure before hardware prototyping. Export SysML models to Cameo for integration into formal verification workflows. Run Monte Carlo campaigns to determine sparing requirements for long‑duration missions—directly addressing gaps in current reliability modeling. Leverage 2025 CHAPEA behavioral data for human-autonomy teaming validation.

### 10.2 For Systems Engineering Students

Hands‑on experience with closed‑loop dynamics, entropy budgeting, and failure cascade analysis. Time‑travel replay turns every collapse into a learning opportunity. Access to the same validation datasets used by NASA (HI‑SEAS, CHAPEA).

### 10.3 For Human‑Autonomy Teaming Researchers

The platform is built for A/B experiments at scale. Every override, regret trajectory, and trust evolution is logged. Researchers can deploy different Governor policies to different cohorts and receive auto‑generated ANOVA reports, generating publishable data on how humans calibrate trust in autonomous systems. CHAPEA Mission 2 (2025) and Mission 3 (2026) data will provide the most current calibration baselines. 

### 10.4 For Physicists and Life‑Support Scientists

The Component Card system allows uploading custom hardware models and testing them in an integrated colony context. Validate against published benchmarks or upload field data for RMSE comparison—V-HAB and EcosimPro have proven this capability. The 2025 Sabatier water quality data enables realistic ISRU modeling. 

### 10.5 For Mission Architects

What redundancy is essential? Where are the single points of failure? The cyber‑physical risk graph and Monte Carlo analysis provide quantitative answers, not speculation. This addresses long‑standing gaps in understanding current ECLSS reliability for future missions. The 2025 radiation shielding findings (aluminum increases surface dose; regolith has optimal thickness) directly inform habitat design decisions. 

